The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (aggregated_bytes > max_bytes)
    return false;

if (packet_num >= BATADV_MAX_AGGREGATION_PACKETS)
    return false;
```
Reason:
These checks prevent aggregation based on size limits (MTU/max bytes) and packet count, but they do not verify whether the existing skb has enough tailroom to accommodate the new packet. When OGM aggregation state is toggled at runtime, a forwarded packet may have been allocated with only `packet_len` bytes. Later, a larger packet can still pass these checks, allowing the aggregation to proceed and causing a buffer overflow in `skb_put`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+	if (skb_tailroom(forw_packet->skb) < packet_len)
+		return false;
```
Reason:
This added check ensures that the existing skb has enough tailroom to append the new packet before aggregation is allowed. If the tailroom is insufficient, the function returns false, causing the caller to create a new forward packet instead of appending, thus preventing the `skb_put` overflow.

